  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev16-05",
    "question": "<p>(a) Use the results for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n} r\"></span> <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n} r^{2}\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n} r^{3}\"></span> , to prove that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n} r(r+1)(r+5)=\\frac{1}{4} n(n+1)(n+2)(n+7)\"></span> for all positive integers n. <span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p><p>(b) Hence, or otherwise, find the value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=20}^{50} r(r+1)(r+5)\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>Expanding <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r(r+1)(r+5)\"></span> gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"r^3+6r^2+5r\"></span>. All three standard sums have a factor <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n(n+1)\"></span>, so take it out before trying to factor the rest. The range 20 through 50 includes 31 terms and starts immediately after the sum through 19.</p>",
      "<p>(a) Let <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T_j=\\sum_{r=1}^n r^j\"></span>. Expanding the summand gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_n=5 T_{1} + 6 T_{2} + T_{3}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Substitute <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T_3=\\frac{n^{2} \\left(n + 1\\right)^{2}}{4}\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T_2=\\frac{n \\left(n + 1\\right) \\left(2 n + 1\\right)}{6}\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T_1=\\frac{n \\left(n + 1\\right)}{2}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Consequently <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_n=\\frac{n^{2} \\left(n + 1\\right)^{2}}{4} + n \\left(n + 1\\right) \\left(2 n + 1\\right) + \\frac{5 n \\left(n + 1\\right)}{2}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Simplifying and factorising gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_n=\\frac{n \\left(n + 1\\right) \\left(n + 2\\right) \\left(n + 7\\right)}{4}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) Subtract the partial sums <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_{50}-S_{19}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>This gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\left(1889550\\right)-\\left(51870\\right)=1837680\"></span>.  <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 7 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev16-05-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for Jan 2011 · Q5, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev16-06",
    "question": "<p>(a) Prove by induction that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n} r^{2}=\\frac{1}{6} n(n+1)(2n+1)\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(6)</span></p><p>Using the standard results for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n} r\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n} r^{2},\"></span></p><p>(b) show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n} (r+2)(r+3)=\\frac{1}{3} n(n^{2}+an+b),\"></span></p><p>where a and b are integers to be found. <span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p><p>(c) Hence show that <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=n+1}^{2n} (r+2)(r+3)=\\frac{1}{3} n(7n^{2}+27n+26)\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>For the induction step extract <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k+1\"></span> after adding its square. The residual numerator <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k(2k+1)+6(k+1)=2k^2+7k+6\"></span> factors as <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(k+2)(2k+3)\"></span>. This yields exactly the sum-of-squares formula at <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k+1\"></span>.</p>",
      "<p>(a) At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=1\"></span>, both sides equal <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Assume <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_k=\\frac{k \\left(k + 1\\right) \\left(2 k + 1\\right)}{6}\"></span> for a positive integer <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Adding the next term gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_{k+1}=\\frac{k \\left(k + 1\\right) \\left(2 k + 1\\right)}{6}+\\left(k + 1\\right)^{2}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Collecting terms gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_{k+1}=\\frac{\\left(k + 1\\right) \\left(k + 2\\right) \\left(2 k + 3\\right)}{6}\"></span>, the required formula with <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=k+1\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>The base case and inductive implication prove the formula for every positive integer. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) Let <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T_j=\\sum_{r=1}^n r^j\"></span>. Expanding the summand gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_n=6 T_{0} + 5 T_{1} + T_{2}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Substitute <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T_2=\\frac{n \\left(n + 1\\right) \\left(2 n + 1\\right)}{6}\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T_1=\\frac{n \\left(n + 1\\right)}{2}\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T_0=n\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Consequently <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_n=\\frac{n \\left(n + 1\\right) \\left(2 n + 1\\right)}{6} + \\frac{5 n \\left(n + 1\\right)}{2} + 6 n\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Simplifying and factorising gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_n=\\frac{n \\left(n^{2} + 9 n + 26\\right)}{3}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Hence <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"a=9,\\ b=26\"></span>.</p>",
      "<p>(c) Subtract the partial sums <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_{2 n}-S_{n}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>This gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\left(\\frac{2 n \\left(4 n^{2} + 18 n + 26\\right)}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{n \\left(n^{2} + 9 n + 26\\right)}{3}\\right)=\\frac{n \\left(n + 2\\right) \\left(7 n + 13\\right)}{3}\"></span>.  <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>"
    ],
    "markSchemeSteps": [
      "<p>Retained exercise: 14 marks.</p>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev16-06-ms-01.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2010 · Q9, extract 1\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev16-06-ms-02.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2010 · Q9, extract 2\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"exam-solution-source\" style=\"margin:2mm 0\"><img src=\"resources/old-spec/rev16-06-ms-03.webp\" alt=\"Source marking table for June 2010 · Q9, extract 3\" style=\"display:block;margin:auto;height:auto;object-fit:contain;width:100%;max-height:210mm\"></figure>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "old-spec/rev16-07",
    "question": "<p>(a) Prove by induction that, for any positive integer n,</p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n} r^{3}=\\frac{1}{4} n^{2}(n+1)^{2}\"></div><p><span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p><p>(b) Using the formulae for <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n} r\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n} r^{3}\"></span> , show that</p><div class=\"equation\" data-display=\"true\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=1}^{n} \\left(r^{3}+3 r+2\\right)=\\frac{1}{4} n(n+2)\\left(n^{2}+7\\right)\"></div><p><span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></p><p>(c) Hence evaluate <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\sum_{r=15}^{25} \\left(r^{3}+3 r+2\\right)\"></span></p><p><span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></p>",
    "steps": [
      "<p>The sum of cubes equals the square of a triangular number. In the induction step the next cube contributes <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"4(k+1)\"></span> after factoring out <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"(k+1)^2/4\"></span>. Later, to keep terms 15 through 25, remove the first fourteen terms of the appropriate polynomial sum.</p>",
      "<p>(a) At <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=1\"></span>, both sides equal <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"1\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Assume <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_k=\\frac{k^{2} \\left(k + 1\\right)^{2}}{4}\"></span> for a positive integer <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Adding the next term gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_{k+1}=\\frac{k^{2} \\left(k + 1\\right)^{2}}{4}+\\left(k + 1\\right)^{3}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Collecting terms gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_{k+1}=\\frac{\\left(k + 1\\right)^{2} \\left(k + 2\\right)^{2}}{4}\"></span>, the required formula with <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"n=k+1\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>The base case and inductive implication prove the formula for every positive integer. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>(b) Let <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T_j=\\sum_{r=1}^n r^j\"></span>. Expanding the summand gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_n=2 T_{0} + 3 T_{1} + T_{3}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Substitute <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T_3=\\frac{n^{2} \\left(n + 1\\right)^{2}}{4}\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T_1=\\frac{n \\left(n + 1\\right)}{2}\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"T_0=n\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[2]</small></p>",
      "<p>Consequently <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_n=\\frac{n^{2} \\left(n + 1\\right)^{2}}{4} + \\frac{3 n \\left(n + 1\\right)}{2} + 2 n\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>Simplifying and factorising gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_n=\\frac{n \\left(n + 2\\right) \\left(n^{2} + 7\\right)}{4}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>(c) Subtract the partial sums <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"S_{25}-S_{14}\"></span>. <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>",
      "<p>This gives <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\left(106650\\right)-\\left(11368\\right)=95282\"></span>.  <small class=\"step-credit\">[1]</small></p>"
    ],
